              heading: 'Part 19',
              text: 'Over the last few decades, the limits of the approach and methods of the Green Revolution have become visible — the long-term impoverishment of the soil, depletion of groundwater, contamination of the soil and water by pesticides and fertilisers, damage to human and animal health (including Fig. 1.18. M.S. Swaminathan, architect of the Green Revolution, recipient of Bharat Ratna Fig. 1.19. Stamp released to mark the revolution in wheat production in India. Note the bar graph in the stamp. Exploring Society: India and Beyond Grade Part multiplication of cancer cases in rural areas, as many scientific studies have established), among others. More and more agricultural scientists the world over question the sustainability of this type of agriculture. Sustainable pathways In recent years, there has been a growing interest in combining the productivity of modern methods with the wisdom of traditional practices to create sustainable agriculture. This approach is inspired by Indian Knowledge Systems (IKS) and focuses on environment-friendly, long-lasting farming practices. One example is the resurgence of organic farming, which avoids the use of chemical fertilisers and pesticides. Instead, it relies on natural fertilisers, such as compost, seed cake (residue after oil has been extracted from seeds) and manure from animal sources. Another example is the use of neem-based pesticides, which are natural and much less harmful to the environment than chemical ones. DON’T MISS OUT Sikkim is the world’s first per cent organic state. It adopted an organic farming model for sustainable agriculture by banning in 2014 chemical fertilisers and pesticides, and focusing on natural farming practices. (We visited Sikkim in Part of this textbook.) LET’S EXPLORE Identify modern agricultural solutions inspired by traditional practices (for example., neem-based practices).',
